      "generation": {
        "attempted": true,
        "success": true,
        "provider": "groq/openai/gpt-oss-20b",
        "answer": "Hybrid search is a retrieval strategy that **combines sparse keyword\u2011based methods (e.g., BM25) with dense embedding\u2011based similarity** so that a query can be matched both exactly on terms and semantically (Hybrid Search | Section: Hybrid Search).  \n\nIt matters because it **improves answer quality**:  \n* It disambiguates queries that contain specific keywords, acronyms, or names that pure embeddings might blur together (Hybrid Search | Section: When It Helps Most).  \n* In small corpora, dense retrieval can return near\u2011ties; the keyword overlap from sparse retrieval can resolve those ties (Hybrid Search | Section: When It Helps Most).  \n* In a Retrieval\u2011Augmented Generation pipeline, hybrid search helps ground responses in real source text, reducing hallucination and enabling the model to answer questions about private or recent data it was never trained on (Retrieval\u2011Augmented Generation | Section: Why RAG Matters).\n\nSources: (Note: Hybrid Search), (Note: Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)), (Note: Agentic AI Concepts)",
        "citation_count": 3,
